{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This shall be the law of the metzora, etc.</b> Scripture called him 'metzora' — implying he is still a metzora — yet how can it say 'on the day of his purification,' for if he is a metzora there is no day of his purification? And further, how did it call it 'the day of his purification' before it said 'and behold, it is healed,' for before healing there is no day of his purification? And what is the meaning of 'and he shall be brought to the priest,' for 'and he shall be brought' implies against his will? And further, afterward it contradicts this and says: 'and the priest shall go outside the camp' — implying that he is not brought to the priest, but rather the priest goes to him. And further, what is the meaning of 'and behold, the plague of tzara'at is healed from the metzora' — it opened with two terms and concluded with one, and it should have said 'from the one afflicted with tzara'at.'" "And the explanation of the matter is as I explained above in parashat Tazria: that tzara'at comes upon known sins, and the name of the affliction is 'tzara'at' and the name of the afflicted one is 'metzora,' for the expression metzora means motzi ra — one who brings out evil, who expels all his hidden evil from within him to the outside, to expose his wickedness in public. And the name tzara'at is an expression of tzara ra'ah, meaning an evil that is like a foe (tzarah), as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥagigah 5a): ״וּמְצָאוּהוּ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת וְצָרוֹת״ — they are like foes to one another, like a wasp and a scorpion. For just as this affliction has no remedy by way of nature, so this tzara'at has no remedy by way of nature, to make known that the hand of HaShem has done this. And the tzara'at of Na'aman proves this, for the physician-sages despaired of seeking a remedy for him. And if so, of necessity the sinner will turn back to his heart to turn away from his evil path. And 'nega' is the name of the sin, for it touches the honor of Heaven, as it were, or the honor of creatures. And the tzara'at compels him that against his will, not of his own good, he is brought to the priest to seek from him a remedy, to learn the discipline of understanding. For had he gone initially of his own will to seek Torah from the lips of a priest and to be among the disciples of Aaron, loving peace and pursuing peace, and not to send strife between brothers with the whip of his tongue, then he would not have come to this tzara'at. And since he did not do this of his own will while he was healthy — אוּלָם (Psalms 73:4) — behold now 'and he shall be brought to the priest' against his will, to seek from him the way of repentance." "And this is what it says: 'This shall be the law of the metzora on the day of his purification.' For on the day that he renews a pure heart within him and accepts upon himself to return in repentance, on that very day he is brought against his will to the priest to learn from him the way of HaShem. And the expression 'and he shall be brought' does not speak of a transfer from place to place, but rather of the transfer of his heart from the evil path in which he walked to the good path — that is, that he accepts upon himself to draw near to the priest and to seek Torah from his mouth. And concerning this it says 'this shall be the law of' — and afterward, once he has accepted upon himself to be pure of thoughts and pure of eyes from seeing evil, then 'and the priest shall go outside the camp, and behold, the nega is healed' — that sin which caused him the tzara'at — 'from the metzora,' from his deficiency. And it could not say 'from the one afflicted (ha-nagua'),' for the sin of his own deeds caused it to him, and it was necessary to say 'from the metzora (ha-tzaru'a),' to indicate that the spiritual healing came to him from the very fact that he was a metzora, for the tzara'at compelled him to repentance; and even so, the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts his repentance, even though it was done under compulsion. And the deficiency involved in such repentance will be found explained further below in parashat Vayeilekh, with the help of HaShem, on the verse ״וּמְצָאוּהוּ רָעוֹת רַבּוֹת וְצָרוֹת״ — see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:41.921701395Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}